edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186505865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186505870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186505872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1084 #edb/ref 17592186505895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1085 "A workshop where cars are painted has ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ liters of paint available to paint some parts of a customer's car." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1086 #uuid "80b67815-157b-5586-a817-27b378ea39d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505728 #edb/ref 1087 #edb/ref 17592186505853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505729 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505729 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166823" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505729 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505729 #edb/ref 1071 "The painter's customer paid the painter $$247.63$ over $4$ days. Estimate the amount of money the customer paid the painter each day by rounding the total amount paid to the nearest dollar.\n\nThe customer paid approximately $\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505729 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0001b715-e657-523a-aa88-1e45abcade35" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505729 #edb/ref 1074 "To estimate the amount of money the customer paid for each day the painter worked, we need to find an approximation for $247.63÷4.$\n\nSo, we round the dividend to the nearest whole number:\n\n$$\n247.63\\to 248\n$$\n\nNow, we divide $248$ by $4$ as usual:\n\n$$\n248÷4=62\n$$\n\nTherefore, the customer paid approximately $$62$ per day." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505756 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505756 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166822" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505756 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505756 #edb/ref 1071 "In preparation for his entire week's work, the painter purchased $8\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ liters of thinner. Of this amount, he used $7\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ liters. How much thinner did he have left? Express your answer as a mixed fraction.\n\nThe painter had$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of thinner left over." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505756 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34a9113a-d903-5269-9893-62edd17b6695" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505756 #edb/ref 1074 "The painter had $(8\\,\\frac{5}{6}-7\\,\\frac{2}{3})$ liters of thinner left over.\n\nWriting $8\\,\\frac{5}{6}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n8\\,\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{8\\times 6+5}{6}=\\frac{53}{6}.\n$$\n\nWriting $7\\,\\frac{2}{3}$ as an improper fraction, we have\n\n$$\n7\\,\\frac{2}{3}=\\frac{7\\times 3+2}{3}=\\frac{23}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our problem now is to work out the following:\n\n$$\n\\frac{53}{6}-\\frac{23}{3}\n$$\n\nTo subtract two fractions with unlike denominators, we need to express each fraction as an equivalent fraction with a common denominator.\n\nIn this question, we can make a common denominator of $6.$\n\nTo put $\\frac{23}{3}$ over a denominator of $6,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{23}{3}=\\frac{23\\times 2}{3\\times 2}=\\frac{46}{6}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\frac{53}{6}-\\frac{46}{6}=\\frac{7}{6}\n$$\n\nFinally, we convert back to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{6}=1\\,\\text{R}\\,1=1\\,\\frac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the painter had $1\\,\\frac{1}{6}$ liters of thinner left over." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505774 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505615 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505774 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166820" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505774 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505774 #edb/ref 1071 "To apply the first coat of paint, the painter mixes $0.4$ liters of paint with $0.24$ liters of thinner. What is the volume of the combined mixture?\n\nExpressed as a decimal, the volume of the mixture equals$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505774 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e69e515-4033-521d-8eb1-22e2f3e1c89a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505774 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the volume of the mixture, we need to calculate the sum $0.4+0.24.$\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $0.4$ and $2$ decimal places in $0.24.$\n\nTo add the numbers, they both need to have the same number of decimals. Hence, we rewrite the first number so that it has $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n0.4=0.40\n$$\n\nNext, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by adding the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of the mixture equals $0.64$ liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505801 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505801 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166821" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505801 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505801 #edb/ref 1071 "After applying the first coat of paint, the painter had $0.18$ liters of mixture left over. How much mixture did the painter use when applying the first coat of paint? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nThe painter used$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of the mixture when applying the first coat." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505801 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "aeb902d6-842c-5327-9a4f-f9343ff58a2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505801 #edb/ref 1074 "In part 3, we found that the painter prepared $0.64$ liters of the mixture.\n\nTo determine the amount of mixture the painter used, we need to calculate $0.64-0.18.$\n\nFirst, we line up the decimal points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we proceed by subtracting the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}14\\end{aligned} \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,-\\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,.\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,6\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the painter used $0.46$ liters of the mixture when applying the first coat." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505639 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505807 #edb/ref 1071 "Expressed as an improper fraction, the exact amount of paint available is$\\,${{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b66642ed-0ee4-55e3-841c-23e21fdfce5f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505807 #edb/ref 1074 "The exact amount of paint is ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ liters.\n\nWriting ${(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}$ with multiplication signs, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2} & ={{\\frac{20}{9}\\times \\frac{20}{9}}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}} \\\\  & =\\frac{20\\times 20}{9\\times 9} \\\\  & =\\frac{400}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\frac{20}{9})}^{2}=\\frac{400}{81}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186505681 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-166819" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505817 #edb/ref 1071 "The instructions state that $0.6$ liters of thinner should be used for every liter of paint. How much thinner is needed if the painter plans to use $0.4$ liters of paint? Express your answer as a decimal.\n\nThe painter should use$\\,$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\,$liters of thinner with $0.4$ liters of paint." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c684d185-df16-528a-b856-5845d2d8fecb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505817 #edb/ref 1074 "To determine the amount of thinner the painter should use with $0.4$ liters of paint, we have to multiply $0.6$ by $0.4.$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers.\n\nNotice that the second number has a leading $0$, which we ignore during the multiplication:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.6\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,.4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $0.6$ and there is $1$ decimal place in $0.4.$\nTherefore, their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $24$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places.\n\n$$\n\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,{{24}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nHence, $0.6\\times 0.4=0.24.$\n\nWe conclude that the painter should use $0.24$ liters of thinner in $0.4$ liters of paint." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505853 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505865 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505853 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1aa44725-235d-541c-ba86-0858b141c403" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505853 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505864 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505864 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "49176f4e-dea3-50b1-88df-46ae14cd3269" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505864 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505756 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505865 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505865 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4e76a7e0-6f32-54ca-a9ba-41647771d28c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505865 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505870 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186505895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505870 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5bca0067-1aa8-5564-9737-96529b5aaa94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505870 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505872 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6fe1a753-77fa-5e4d-a46a-6c87b21d40bc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505872 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186505729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505892 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d48a0192-079a-5f95-bf79-195d459c5730" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186505892 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 175921865057